mb|1C|MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BNorth: ^*nCan I overcall 1@H with only 4 hearts?|
PG| ***** 54 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|You can't overcall 1@H with a bad 4-card suit, But you can make a takeout double. I personally prefer to never overcall a 4-card suit. However, these days many players will overcall when they have a 4-card suit with 3 honors.|
PG| ***** 55 ***** |


mb|D|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I don't mind pass by East, though another possible bid would be a 3@C preemptive raise. East is a little weak, especially vulnerable but has nice distribution. Anyway I think pass is okay, but 3@C is not out of the question.|
PG| ***** 56 ***** |


MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|My choices over a double are the cheapest bid in a suit to show no more than 8 points, a jump in a suit showing an invitational hand (good 8 to 11), and a cue bid in the opponents suit that shows an opening hand and is a game force. Since I want to force to game I will cue bid 2@C.|
PG| ***** 57 ***** |


mb|2C|an|artificial gf|
pg| ***** 58 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
mb|2N|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With my club void, I was thinking that 6@H or 6@D was very possible. But since partner bid 2N, showing club values, my hand is not as good. So I will bid 3@H in case we have a heart fit and we will end up in 4@H or 3N.|
PG| ***** 59 ***** |


